 # Hierarchical Data Structure Storage
 
-    Also called: Octree storage layout
+Also called: Octree storage layout
 
-    ## Overview
-    Hierarchical Data Structure Storage is a method for compactly storing precomputed illumination samples in a spatial hierarchy to accelerate volume rendering in participating media [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently querying and traversing large sets of illumination data by organizing samples into a tree structure stored in 1D buffers [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Bottom-up Octree Construction](1590-bottom-up-octree-construction.md), [Packed Tensor Representation](1611-packed-tensor-representation.md), [Octree Volume Structure](1978-octree-volume-structure.md), [Octree Volume Compression](1979-octree-volume-compression.md), [Sparse Voxel Octree Attribute Encoding (Neighbor)](2641-sparse-voxel-octree-attribute-encoding-neighbor.md), [SVO Node Encoding](2705-svo-node-encoding.md), [Sparse Voxel Octree Structure Encoding/Decoding](3012-sparse-voxel-octree-structure-encoding-decoding.md), and [Sparse Volume Data Structures](6804-sparse-volume-data-structures.md) by maintaining two separate, redundant tree structures—one for single scattering and one for multiple scattering—to maximize data locality during traversal [S1].
+## Overview
+Hierarchical Data Structure Storage is a method for organizing illumination samples in participating media into a spatial hierarchy to accelerate rendering when lighting conditions change [S1]. It solves the problem of efficiently storing and traversing complex scattering data by grouping samples into a tree structure, allowing for fast updates and localized recomputation [S1]. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Packed Tensor Representation](1611-packed-tensor-representation.md) by its explicit separation of single and multiple scattering into two parallel tree structures, which increases data locality at the cost of structural redundancy [S1].
 
-    ## How it works
-    **Inputs:** A set of illumination samples computed for current lighting conditions [S1]. Each sample consists of position, direction, incoming radiance, scaled surface area, and interval length along the ray [S1].
-    **Outputs:** Two 1D buffer arrays representing the single-scattering and multiple-scattering hierarchies, respectively [S1].
+## How it works
+**Inputs:** Illumination samples computed for current lighting conditions.
