+## Subtopics
 
-    **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-    * **Redundancy:** The method stores the hierarchy twice (once for single, once for multiple scattering) [S1]. This introduces memory redundancy but is a deliberate trade-off to increase data locality and reduce traversal time [S1].
-    * **Lighting Changes:** The hierarchy is only valid for the specific lighting conditions under which it was computed [S1]. Any change in lighting requires a full recomputation of samples and rebuilding of the hierarchies [S1].
-    * **Traversal Details:** The source describes a "Two-Steps Hierarchy Traversal" algorithm that extracts a coarse subtree from the root before restarting traversal, but the full algorithm text is truncated in the provided evidence [S1].
+### LCC Format Family
+An industrial-grade data format family evolved from LCC1 to LCC2 [S2, unofficial]. LCC1 supports spatial chunking and Level-of-Detail (LoD) organization for progressive loading and large-scale scene visualization [S2, unofficial]. LCC2 decouples data storage from LoD partitioning, using a flexible hierarchical LoD tree to index different payload types and storage backends [S2, unofficial]. Combined with compact binary layout and parameter quantization, it reduces storage by up to 90% compared to standard PLY files [S2, unofficial].
 
-    ## Operating characteristics
-    * **Memory:** Uses approximately double the memory for the tree structure compared to a single hierarchy due to the separation of single and multiple scattering data [S1]. The raw sample storage uses a Structure of Arrays, which requires grouping samples in eights [S1].
-    * **Time Complexity:** Traversal is optimized via a "Two-Steps Hierarchy Traversal" method (coarse subtree extraction followed by fine traversal), which is the main computational cost [S1]. The storage format (1D buffer with AABB and indices) allows for efficient cache-friendly traversal [S1].
-    * **Strengths:** High data locality for scattering computations; fast traversal due to optimized two-step algorithm; compact storage of bounding boxes and indices [S1].
-    * **Limitations:** High memory usage due to redundancy; requires full rebuild on lighting changes; limited to homogeneous participating media with refractive boundaries as per the source context [S1].
+## Operating characteristics
+*   **Time Complexity:** Building the hierarchy requires computing illumination samples and traversing the tree to aggregate node values. Traversal is optimized via a two-step process (coarse subtree extraction followed by fine traversal), reducing the main computational cost [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** The method stores two full copies of the tree structure (one for single, one for multiple scattering), doubling the memory overhead for the hierarchy itself compared to a single tree [S1]. The leaf data is stored in a compact SoA format.
+*   **Strengths:** High data locality during scattering computation due to separate trees for different scattering orders [S1]. Efficient updates for static lighting scenarios [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Redundant storage of the tree structure [S1]. Requires rebuilding the hierarchy when lighting changes [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering participating media with complex single and multiple scattering where lighting changes are infrequent, and data locality is critical for performance [S1].
 
-    ## Background
-    The method was introduced in the paper "Point-Based Rendering for Homogeneous Participating Media with Refractive Boundaries" [S1]. It is part of a point-based rendering approach for volume rendering, specifically designed to handle complex lighting interactions in participating media by precomputing and hierarchically storing illumination samples [S1].
